steps.body:
A `switch` compares one value against a list of constants and jumps straight to the matching `case`. It reads better than a long `else if` chain when every branch tests the same variable for equality.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const int level = 2;
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
switch (level) {
    case 1:
        std::cout << "Easy\n";
        break;
    case 2:
        std::cout << "Hard\n";
        break;
    default:
        std::cout << "Unknown\n";
        break;
}
```

- The value in `switch (...)` must be an integer, a `char`, or an enum. A `double` does not compile.
- Each `case` label must be a constant known at compile time, such as `3`, `'x'`, or a `const int` set from a literal. A plain variable is not allowed, and two labels cannot repeat.
- `break` jumps out of the switch.
- `default` runs when no case matches. It is optional, but it is the place to catch bad values.

**Gotcha:** a case with no `break` falls through and keeps running the next case's code too. Forgetting one prints two answers. End every case with `break` unless you mean it.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int day = 3;
    std::cout << "Day " << day << ": ";
    switch (day) {
        case 1:
            std::cout << "Monday\n";
            break;
        case 2:
            std::cout << "Tuesday\n";
            break;
        case 3:
            std::cout << "Wednesday\n";
            break;
        case 4:
            std::cout << "Thursday\n";
            break;
        case 5:
            std::cout << "Friday\n";
            break;
        case 6:
            std::cout << "Saturday\n";
            break;
        case 7:
            std::cout << "Sunday\n";
            break;
        default:
            std::cout << "Invalid day\n";
            break;
    }
    return 0;
}

