steps.body:
Stack several labels on one body when they should do the same thing. A switch on a `char` uses char literals as labels.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const char answer = 'Y';
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
switch (answer) {
    case 'y':
    case 'Y':
        std::cout << "Yes\n";
        break;
    default:
        std::cout << "No\n";
        break;
}
```

Sometimes you want one case to run its code and then continue into the next. Mark that on purpose with the C++17 attribute `[[fallthrough]];` as the last statement before the next label. It changes nothing at run time, but it tells readers and the compiler (with `-Wimplicit-fallthrough`) that the missing `break` is not a bug.

A case that declares a variable needs its own braces. Without them, the variable's scope runs into the later cases, and jumping past its initialization is a compile error.

```cpp harness
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const char grade = 'A';
    switch (grade) {
        {{code}}
            std::cout << "Passed\n";
            break;
        default:
            std::cout << "Try again\n";
            break;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
case 'A': {
    const int stars = 3;
    std::cout << stars << '\n';
    [[fallthrough]];
}
case 'B':
```

**Gotcha:** `switch` cannot take a `std::string`. Use an `if` / `else if` chain to compare strings.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const char grade = 'A';
    std::cout << "Grade: " << grade << '\n';
    switch (grade) {
        case 'A': {
            const int stars = 3;
            std::cout << "Excellent, " << stars << " stars\n";
            [[fallthrough]];
        }
        case 'B':
        case 'C':
            std::cout << "Passed\n";
            break;
        case 'D':
        case 'F':
            std::cout << "Try again\n";
            break;
        default:
            std::cout << "Unknown grade\n";
            break;
    }
    return 0;
}

